    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the left hindfoot (M19.172)</content>. Partial relief after four weeks; pain still above 4 of 10. Ultrasound-guided subtalar injection done today with his consent: pain fell from 6 to 1 of 10 on walking within 15 minutes, which confirms the subtalar joint as the main source. He was told the steroid may raise his blood glucose for a few days. [S]<list><item>Continue the brace, home exercises, naproxen with food and diclofenac gel.</item><item>Rides from the Community Health Representative program for physical therapy, and video sessions on weeks he cannot travel.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Prediabetes (R73.03)</content>. Working on diet with the diabetes prevention program; the steroid injection may raise glucose briefly. [S]<list><item>Hemoglobin A1c at about four months.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain on walking (0 to 10 numeric rating scale)</td><td>Every visit and after each treatment trial</td><td>Completed today</td><td>5 of 10 before the injection, from 7</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Function: Foot and Ankle Ability Measure, activities of daily living subscale</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>64%, from 52%</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Joint examination: hindfoot and ankle motion, alignment, swelling and gait</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Subtalar motion painful; less talonavicular tenderness; skin intact under the brace</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise, physical therapy and brace use</td><td>Every visit; physical therapy weekly for six weeks</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Two of four physical therapy sessions were missed because he had no ride to a clinic an hour away. No harm resulted: he kept up the home exercises and his pain and function improved. Corrective action: rides through the Community Health Representative program and video sessions when he cannot travel, as the consultant had advised if travel became hard</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>NSAID safety: blood pressure, creatinine and blood count; stomach symptoms</td><td>Before an oral NSAID, then every 6 to 12 months on long-term use</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Four weeks of naproxen; no stomach symptoms; blood pressure 126/78</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Imaging</td><td>At diagnosis or for surgical planning; not for routine follow-up</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No change in symptoms that would call for new images</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood lead with retained bullet fragments</td><td>Once when fragments are found; repeat only if fragments lie in a joint or bone, or symptoms suggest lead toxicity</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Fragments outside the joints and blood lead low at the last visit</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, with home readings to confirm or exclude hypertension</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>126/78 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screening, sleep and coping</td><td>At baseline and periodically</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 6 at intake; sleeping better as pain eases</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hemoglobin A1c in prediabetes</td><td>At diagnosis, then in three to four months and yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>6.0% at intake; due at about four months</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Food access and the food distribution program</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Application for the food distribution program submitted</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Traditional healing alongside clinic care, at his choice</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>His choice; nothing asked</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Walk a mile on uneven ground and ride with pain of 3 or less. In progress; target 2026-09-14 [S]</item><item>Decide about surgery with full information, in his own time. In progress; target 2026-09-14 [S]</item><item>Bring hemoglobin A1c below 5.7%. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>Enough food through the month. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis and depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monographs (2026); Indian Health Manual, Part 3, Chapter 30: Chronic Non-Cancer Pain Management (2026); Special General Memorandum 94-08: Statement of Policy for the Traditional Cultural Advocacy Program (1994); Community Health Representative Program (2026); Prediabetes and Type 2 Diabetes: Screening (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Ten-week review in five weeks. Physical therapy weekly with rides arranged. Call for redness, swelling or fever after the injection. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-14: ten-week review after the injection: pain, function, surgery discussed and declined for now. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
